  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Mid-lvl vorticity continues to lift northeast \nthrough the Tri-state area early this afternoon. Scattered showers \nand thunderstorms tied to this wave will continue to slide east \nnortheast into the afternoon hours.  Most of the strongest activity \nhas been tied to the NW quadrant of the wave, aligned with an \napproaching cool front  moving in from the northwest. Some of the \nconvection has produced rainfall rates greater than 3-4\" per hour, \nwith radar estimates suggesting 2-4\" of rain has fallen in isolated \npockets in Miner county. Convection risks will remain focused mostly \non heavy rain this afternoon, though if sufficient clearing can \ndevelop in SW Minnesota and NW Iowa, then a small risk of 50 mph \nwinds, small hail, and also brief funnels could be possible given \nthe weak and chaotic surface flow. The heavy rain risk will persist \nin any thunderstorm given deep warm cloud layer, and PWATs above the \n90th percentile of climatology. \n\nTONIGHT: The front clears the CWA by late evening, with skies \nbriefly clearing.  Those skies may only stay clear for a short \nduration of time as increasing mid-uper forcing could bring high lvl \nclouds back into the region.  If these clouds don't develop, then \novernight lows could fall into the upper 50s to lower 60s, inducing \nsome fog potential.  \n\nSUNDAY: A dry day is anticipated for Sunday as winds remain \nnortherly.  Temperatures rise into the lower 80s in most locations, \nwith a bit of mid-high lvl cloud cover in the region through the day \nand diurnal CU in the afternoon. \n\nMONDAY: Northwesterly flow will persist into the new work week \nand is expected to bring a frontal boundary into the Dakotas on \nMonday. An initial surge of warm advection Sunday night into \nMonday could bring scattered showers and thunderstorms eastward\nout of central South Dakota/Nebraska by daybreak. This activity\nmay slowly fade through the morning allowing stronger diurnal \nheating to occur. The frontal boundary arriving in the afternoon\nwill then be watched for convection risks. Should convergence \nalong this boundary be strong enough to overcome lingering \ninhibition, isolated to scattered storms may develop by 3-5pm as\nsoundings show a relatively uncapped environment through \neastern SODAK. Upwards of 2000-2500 J/KG MLCAPE ahead of the \nboundary, may combine with modest effective shear approaching 35\nknots to help produce a few severe storms with 2\" hail and 60 \nmph wind potential. The overall coverage of storms remain most \nuncertain given unknown impacts of morning clouds. \n\nTUESDAY: Monday's convection may dictate the placement of the \nfrontal boundary by Tuesday. Some guidance pushes the front and \nboundary layer moisture south of the CWA, with other models holding \nthe effective boundary closer to I-90. The NW flow will likely bring \nanother wave into the Plains in the afternoon, which could trigger \nisolated to scattered strong late afternoon and evening storms once \nagain.  \n\nWEDNESDAY-FRIDAY: With the mid-lvl ridge centered back over the Four \nCorners region we'll continue to see northwesterly flow aloft \nthrough the Plains for the second half of the week. This problematic \nflow pattern will keep a rain risk into the region Wednesday and \npotentially Thursday, before the ridge sharpens and pushes more \nstable air into the Plains into next weekend. Expect lower than \ntypical confidence in specific forecast details during the middle of \nthe week.  Temperatures through the second half of the week will run \nnear to slightly below their seasonal normals which are now in the \nlower 80s.",
